problems.problem:
A number $p$ is called perfect if the sum of its divisors, except $p$ itself, gives $p$ as result. Let $f$ be a function such that:
$f(n)=0$ if $n$ is perfect;
$f(n)=0$ if the units digit of $n$ is $4$;
$f(a\cdot b)=f(a)+f(b)$.
Compute $f(1988)$.

The Mayor of a city wishes to establish a transport system with at least one bus line, in which:
(i) each line passes through exactly three stops;
(ii) every two distinct lines have exactly one stop in common;
(iii) for every two distinct bus stops there is exactly one line that passes through both.
Determine the number of bus stops of the city.

There is a sequence $a_1,a_2,a_3,\ldots,a_n,\ldots$ of positive integers with the following properties:
i) every positive integer appears one or more times in the sequence;
ii) $a_1=1$;
iii) $a_{3n+1}=2a_n+1$;
iv) $a_{n+1}\geq a_n$;
v) $a_{2001}=200$.
Compute the value of $a_{1000}$.

Let $g$ be a function defined for every positive integer $n$ that satisfies:
i) $g(1)=1$;
ii) $g(n+1)=g(n)+1$ or $g(n+1)=g(n)-1$ for every $n\geq 1$;
iii) $g(3n)=g(n)$ for every $n\geq 1$;
iv) $g(k)=2001$ for some positive integer $k$.
Find the smallest possible value of $k$ among all the functions $g$ satisfying the above conditions, and prove that it is the smallest.

Prove that there exists a sequence of positive integers $x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n,\ldots$ that satisfies the following two conditions:
(i) it contains each of the positive integers exactly once;
(ii) for each $n=1,2,\ldots$ the partial sum $x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_n$ is divisible by $n^n$.

Consider the following sequence:
$a_1=$ the last digit of the sum of the digits of the number $2005$;
$a_2=$ the last digit of the sum of the digits of the number $20052005$;
$a_3=$ the last digit of the sum of the digits of the number $200520052005$;
$\ldots$
$a_n=$ the last digit of the sum of the digits of the number $\underbrace{20052005\ldots2005}_{n\text{ times }2005}$.
Compute $a_1+a_2+a_3+\cdots+a_{2005}$.

Determine whether there exists an infinite sequence $a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3,\ldots$ of non-negative integers satisfying the following conditions:
(i) every non-negative integer appears in the sequence exactly once;
(ii) the sequence $$b_n=a_n+n,\qquad n\geq 0,$$ consists of all the prime numbers, each appearing exactly once.

Let $ABCD$ be a convex quadrilateral. Let $n\geq 2$ be an integer. Prove that there exist $n$ triangles of the same area satisfying all of the following properties:
– their interiors are disjoint, that is, the triangles do not overlap;
– each triangle is contained in $ABCD$ or in its interior;
– the sum of the areas of the triangles is at least $\frac{4n}{4n+1}$ of the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$.

Let $ABC$ be an acute-angled triangle whose circumcentre is $O$. Let $X$ and $Y$ be points such that:
– $\angle XAB=\angle YCB=90^\circ$;
– $\angle ABC=\angle BXA=\angle BYC$;
– $X$ and $C$ are in different half-planes with respect to $AB$;
– $Y$ and $A$ are in different half-planes with respect to $BC$.
Prove that $O$ is the midpoint of $XY$.

In a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ we have:
– $R$ and $S$ are points in the interior of the segments $CD$ and $AB$, respectively, with $AD=CR$ and $BC=AS$;
– $P$ and $Q$ are the midpoints of $DR$ and $SB$, respectively;
– $M$ is the midpoint of $AC$.
Knowing that $\angle MPC+\angle MQA=90^\circ$, prove that $ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral.

We say that a positive integer $M$ with $2n$ digits is hypersquare if it satisfies the following three conditions:
– $M$ is a perfect square;
– the number formed by the first $n$ digits of $M$ is a perfect square;
– the number formed by the last $n$ digits of $M$ is a perfect square and has exactly $n$ digits (it does not start with zero).
Find a hypersquare number with $2000$ digits.

A $4\times 4$ square board is called brasuca if it satisfies all the following conditions:
– each cell contains one of the numbers $0,1,2,3,4$ or $5$;
– the sum of the numbers in each row is $5$;
– the sum of the numbers in each column is $5$;
– the sum of the numbers in each diagonal of four cells is $5$;
– the number written in the upper left cell of the board is less than or equal to the other numbers of the board;
– when the board is divided into four $2\times 2$ squares, in each of them the sum of the four numbers is $5$.
How many brasuca boards are there?

For each positive integer $N$ with an even number $2k$ of digits, the swapped number of $N$ is the number formed when the first $k$ digits of $N$ are moved to the end, without changing their order. For example, if $N=123456$, the number formed by the first half of the digits is $123$, the one formed by the second half is $456$, and the swapped number is $456123$; if $N=2304$, the swapped number of $N$ is $423$. A positive integer $C$ is called cearense if the following three conditions are satisfied:
(i) $C$ has an even number of digits;
(ii) the number formed by the first half of the digits of $C$ and the number formed by the second half of the digits of $C$ are coprime;
(iii) $C$ divides the swapped number of $C$.
Determine the two smallest cearense numbers.
